   "question": "What has been said about immigration levels and skilled migration?",
   "answer": "The context says the government emphasised maintaining proper skill levels in the migration program and balancing skilled migration with family reunion. Skilled migration was described as having a positive economic impact: it boosts living standards, workforce productivity, labour force participation, and contributes significantly to the Commonwealth budget. For example, skilled migration was estimated to contribute about $1.4 billion to the budget over five years. It was also stated that a substantially larger intake focused on skilled migration would involve diluting selection standards\u2014particularly age and skill\u2014and would dilute the economic benefits.\n\nThe context also mentions that the government increased the migration program by 6,000 places, with 5,000 in the skilled stream, making skilled migration about 52 per cent of the program. There are also comments from other speakers who criticised placing only economic value on migrants, stressed the importance of family reunion, and argued that skilled migration should not come at the expense of jobs, apprenticeships, and traineeships for Australians. Some also said more migrants were needed, especially in regional areas, and linked skilled migration with regional development.",
